eed guaranteed board control.\n\nNow is a free play — meaning I can set the pace. If I play BlackJoker now, I will almost certainly win this trick (unless opponent immediately responds with Red Joker, which is unlikely — they’d probably save it). That gives me another turn to play from my remaining cards.\n\nAlternatively, if I play ♥2, I also have high chances of winning (only Red Joker beats it), and I can save BlackJoker for later.\n\nBut between BlackJoker and ♥2, which is safer to play now?\n\nImportant: Single BlackJoker ranks **below** single RedJoker, but above 2. So order is: RedJoker > BlackJoker > 2 > A > ...\n\nSo BlackJoker beats 2, A, K, etc. Only RedJoker beats it.\n\nThus, playing BlackJoker is **more powerful** than playing ♥2.\n\nIf I play BlackJoker:\n- It wins unless opponent has Red Joker.\n- Then I get to lead again with my remaining four cards.\n- I can then play another single, possibly forcing them to use high cards.\n\nThis is ideal — I clear a high card, gain a tempo, and potentially lead again.\n\nWhereas if I play ♥2, opponent could beat it with either BlackJoker or RedJoker — more likely to lose control.\n\nTherefore, **BlackJoker is the strongest single I can play**, and now is the perfect time — free play, no pressure, and I want to seize initiative.\n\nReason: Playing BlackJoker guarantees winning the trick unless the opponent holds the Red Joker (only one card can beat it). This allows me to lead again and continue clearing my hand. Given that both peasants passed earlier, they may be weak in singles, making this an ideal moment to take control. Saving BlackJoker offers little advantage since it’s only useful in singles or rockets (which I cannot make). Using it now maximizes its utility by securing a lead and enabling another move.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["BlackJoker"]}'}
